{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3277","artifact_type":"feature-context","name":"plan/feature-context-bookend-validator-wiring.md@sha256:ce39a14ecb81bb3326163de4566a1c158c6471e5bbb547430088a0a5fedd6ebe"},"owner_reference":"3277","content":"# Feature Context: Bookend Validator Wiring\n\n**Item**: #3277 \u2014 fix: swarm-task-planner does not generate T0/TN bookend tasks for plans with `acceptance-criteria-structured`\n**Type**: defect (per Issue Classification)\n**Scope of this document**: WHAT and WHY only. No implementation approach, signatures, or call-site design \u2014 those belong to the architect stage.\n\n---\n\n## Problem Space\n\n### The observed failure\n\nPlan `Pb31107bf` (created 2026-08-28/29 by `dh:swarm-task-planner` during `/dh:work-backlog-item work --auto #3268`) carries a fully-formed `acceptance-criteria-structured` list of 6 entries \u2014 each with `criterion-id`, `check-command`, `expected-baseline`, and `expected-final` \u2014 and a task list of exactly `T1, T2, T3, T4, T5, T6`, every one of them with `bookend_type: null`. No T0 baseline task, no TN verification task. Both facts are VERIFIED in the item's Fact-Check section against a direct `sam_plan(action=\"read\")` of the plan.\n\nThat combination is precisely what the documented contract forbids. `implement-feature/SKILL.md`'s \"Bookend Task Ordering\" section states the guarantee without qualification: when a plan contains `acceptance-criteria-structured` entries, T0 and TN bookend tasks exist and the dispatch loop needs no special handling. The development-harness README \u00a796 states the same promise to users: \"Bookend tasks run automatically.\"\n\n### Why the promise is not kept\n\nThe item's Root-Cause Analysis establishes a five-claim evidence chain ending in a **code/schema gap**, not an agent-behaviour problem:\n\n- `BookendValidator` exists at `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/dependencies.py:276-391`. It encodes all five structural rules, including Rule 5 \u2014 \"if `acceptance_criteria_structured` is non-empty, both T0 and TN must exist\" \u2014 and returns a human-readable error string for each violation. Independently re-read and confirmed during this pass: the class and its `validate()` rules are present and correct as described.\n- It is exported publicly from `sam_schema/__init__.py` (lines 30 and 71).\n- It is called from nowhere. A grep for `BookendValidator` across the plugin's non-test Python returns exactly three hits: the two export lines and the class definition itself. Independently reproduced during this pass.\n- The plan-write paths that could enforce it \u2014 `dh_core/operations.py::create_plan` (~line 202), `::append_task` (~line 621), `::finalize_plan` (~line 668) \u2014 contain no reference to it. The MCP layer (`sam_schema/server.py::_sam_plan_create`, `_sam_plan_finalize`) and the CLI (`sam_schema/sam_plan.py`) are thin wrappers that route through `operations.py`. All three backends' (`local_yaml.py`, `github_task.py`, `beads.py`) `finalize_plan` implementations are bare state transitions with no structural check.\n\nThe validator is therefore dead at runtime. `swarm-task-planner.md`'s Phase 4 Step 12 \"bookend presence check\" (\u00a7605-612) instructs the agent to verify bookends before registering the plan, but that is a prose instruction to an LLM, not an enforced gate \u2014 and nothing downstream of the agent catches a plan that fails it. The system has documentation asserting a guarantee, a validator implementing the guarantee, unit tests proving the validator correct, and no path by which the two ever meet.\n\n### Why the failure is silent\n\nThe absence of bookends does not surface as an error anywhere. `complete-implementation/SKILL.md`'s Pre-Phase-1 TN Verification Check (lines 300-324) branches on the presence of a `TN-verification` artifact; when it is absent, the mermaid flowchart at line 318 routes to `\"No structured criteria \u2014 proceed to Phase 1\"` and the prose at line 306 explains that it proceeds \"because no artifact owner is addressable.\" This is VERIFIED in the Fact-Check section. The gate cannot distinguish two materially different conditions:\n\n1. The plan genuinely has no structured criteria, so there is nothing to verify.\n2. The plan has structured criteria that were never captured or checked.\n\nBoth look identical from the gate's vantage point, and both pass. The consequence for `Pb31107bf` is that its 6 structured criteria received no independent before/after measurement. Each implementation task's own `verification-steps` duplicate the equivalent check, but those are self-reported by the same agent that made the edit \u2014 not a baseline measured before any edit landed, and not a single pass re-running all six checks together after everything completes, which is the only place a cross-task regression would show up.\n\n### Why CI does not catch it\n\n`tests_sam/test_dependencies.py` contains 462 lines of `BookendValidator` unit coverage (added alongside the class in commit `5b150f7ac`, \"feat(sam-schema): add T0/TN bookend validation for SAM plans\", 2026-03-15). `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml:244` runs it. Those tests pass \u2014 and will keep passing \u2014 regardless of whether anything in the product calls the validator. A green CI run on this suite is evidence about the validator's internal logic only, and is currently being read as evidence about the system's behaviour. That gap is itself part of the defect: the test suite provides false confidence, which is why the item's Acceptance Criteria #5 requires a test that exercises enforcement *through* `operations.py` rather than against the validator in isolation.\n\n---\n\n## Desired Outcome\n\nThe behaviour the item's Expected Behavior section defines:\n\nA plan-write call \u2014 `create_plan`, `finalize_plan`, or `append_task`, arriving via CLI or MCP, against any of the three backends \u2014 whose resulting plan has a non-empty `acceptance-criteria-structured` list and lacks correct bookend structure does not succeed silently. It is rejected, or corrected, before the write persists.\n\nSuccess looks like:\n\n- **Enforcement is real, not aspirational.** The rule stated in three places (README, `implement-feature/SKILL.md`, `swarm-task-planner.md`) becomes a property the system holds rather than a behaviour it hopes an agent exhibits.\n- **Enforcement is single-sourced.** The Impact Radius is explicit that the fix belongs above the backend layer: duplicating the check into `local_yaml.py`, `github_task.py`, and `beads.py` would create three drifting copies of one rule. One shared layer covers CLI, MCP, and all three backends at once.\n- **The empty case is untouched.** Plans with an empty `acceptance-criteria-structured` list have no bookend requirement and behave exactly as they do today (Acceptance Criteria #3). This is not a new universal requirement on all plans; it is a conditional invariant tied to the presence of structured criteria.\n- **The passing case is untouched.** A plan that already has correct bookends succeeds unchanged (Acceptance Criteria #2). The gate is a guard, not a rewrite.\n- **`BookendValidator`'s existing tests become load-bearing.** Its unit suite must continue to pass unmodified (Acceptance Criteria #6) while starting to validate logic that actually executes.\n- **The downstream gate regains its ability to discriminate.** Once bookend presence is guaranteed at registration time, an absent `TN-verification` artifact stops being ambiguous, and `complete-implementation`'s Pre-Phase-1 check can be trusted to mean what it says. Note this is a *consequence* of the fix, not necessarily a change made by it \u2014 see Open Questions.\n\n---\n\n## Stakeholders\n\n| Stakeholder | Relationship to this change |\n|---|---|\n| `dh:swarm-task-planner` | The producer whose output is being constrained. Today its Phase 4 Step 12 self-check is unenforceable prose; after the fix its plan registration can actually fail, which changes its error surface and may require it to react to a rejected write. |\n| `/dh:complete-implementation`'s Pre-Phase-1 TN gate | The consumer harmed today. It silently passes plans it should flag. It is the reason this defect has downstream cost rather than being merely cosmetic. |\n| `/dh:implement-feature`'s dispatch loop | Documents \"no special handling is needed\" for bookend ordering because T0/TN are assumed present. That assumption becomes true. |\n| `t0-baseline-capture` and `tn-verification-gate` agents | Already built and ready to execute bookend tasks; currently never dispatched on affected plans. They gain the work they were written for. |\n| Any future plan author \u2014 human or agent \u2014 writing plans via CLI or MCP on any backend | Newly subject to a validation error they have never seen. This is the group that bears the migration cost. |\n| Maintainers reading CI results | Stop receiving a green signal that means less than it appears to. |\n\n---\n\n## Risks\n\n### 1. Backward compatibility \u2014 in-flight plans (primary risk)\n\nThis is the risk that needs an explicit decision before implementation.\n\nTwenty-one plans currently exist in the SAM plan store. At least one \u2014 `Pb31107bf`, the plan that produced this issue \u2014 has non-empty `acceptance-criteria-structured` and no bookends. If enforcement is hard and applies to `append_task` and `finalize_plan` as well as `create_plan`, then any further write to such a plan starts failing. A plan mid-execution could become un-finalizable, stranding work that was legitimately in progress before the rule existed.\n\nThe item's Dependencies section states \"no schema migrations\" are required, which is true of the *file format* \u2014 no field is added or changed. It is not a statement that no data remediation is needed. Existing plan *content* may violate a rule that was never enforced when that content was written. Those are different questions and the second one is open.\n\nThe exposure is bounded: most of the 21 plans are complete or were never given structured criteria, and only plans that (a) have non-empty structured criteria, (b) lack bookends, and (c) will receive another write are affected. Enumerating that set is a prerequisite to sizing the risk properly, and has not been done.\n\n### 2. Rejecting a write is a destructive-adjacent outcome\n\nA rejected `finalize_plan` on a plan built incrementally through many `append_task` calls leaves the plan stuck in `drafting`. Whether the caller can recover \u2014 by appending the missing bookends and retrying \u2014 depends on whether `append_task` itself is gated. If both `append_task` and `finalize_plan` reject, a plan could become unfixable through the public API, which would be a worse failure than the one being fixed. The interaction between which operations are gated needs to leave a repair path open.\n\n### 3. Correctness of the enforced rule set\n\n`BookendValidator.validate()` enforces five rules, not one. Rule 4 requires TN to depend on *every* non-bookend task ID. A plan whose bookends exist but whose TN dependency list drifted \u2014 for example because tasks were appended after TN was created \u2014 would newly fail. Acceptance Criteria #1 explicitly includes \"correct bookend dependency wiring\" in scope, so this is intended; the risk is that its blast radius on existing plans is larger than the narrower \"missing T0/TN\" framing suggests.\n\n### 4. Generated artifacts must not be hand-edited\n\nThe Impact Radius flags `docs/workflow-layers/*.json`, `docs/workflow-result.json`, `docs/dh-workflow-graph.json`, and `graphify-out/*` as generated snapshots. If `swarm-task-planner.md`'s structure changes as part of this work, the correct action is to run `dh:meta-workflow-graph-refresh`, not to edit those files. This is a process risk, not a design risk, but it has bitten before.\n\n### 5. Documentation drift in the opposite direction\n\nThree documents currently describe the guarantee as if it were already enforced (`README.md`, `implement-feature/SKILL.md`, `docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` P4_BOOKEND flow at ~lines 395-421 and 533-536). After the fix they become accurate. But `swarm-task-planner.md`'s Phase 4 Step 12 self-check now overlaps a real runtime gate \u2014 leaving both without acknowledging the relationship risks a future reader assuming the prose check is the enforcement.\n\n---\n\n## Open Questions\n\nThese require a decision during architecture or planning; this document deliberately does not answer them.\n\n1. **Hard error or warn-then-block rollout?** The Expected Behavior section says a violating plan is \"rejected (or corrected, per the remediation approach chosen during planning)\" \u2014 the choice is explicitly deferred. A hard error is the strongest guarantee and the smallest amount of code, but it converts existing non-conforming plans into failures immediately. A warn-first period surfaces the population of violators without breaking anyone, at the cost of leaving the gap open for that period and needing a second change to close it. A third option the Expected Behavior text hints at \u2014 auto-correcting by synthesising the missing bookend tasks \u2014 avoids the compatibility problem entirely but means a write path silently mutates the caller's plan, which has its own surprise cost.\n\n2. **Does `Pb31107bf` need a backfill pass?** The plan that produced this issue is itself non-conforming and belongs to in-flight item #3268. Options: leave it (accept that #3268 ships without TN verification), backfill bookends into it so it validates and gets real verification, or exempt already-created plans and enforce only on plans created after the fix. This is a product decision about whether #3268's acceptance criteria deserve the empirical check, not a technical one.\n\n3. **Which operations are gated?** Acceptance Criteria #1 names all three of `create_plan`, `append_task`, and `finalize_plan`. But `append_task` on a plan under incremental construction will *always* transiently violate Rule 4 (TN cannot depend on tasks that do not exist yet) and often Rule 5. Gating every append may be incoherent with the incremental build pattern (`create` with empty `tasks` \u2192 `drafting` \u2192 repeated `append_task` \u2192 `finalize`) introduced in #1770. Whether `finalize_plan` is the only coherent gate \u2014 the point at which a plan claims to be complete \u2014 needs deciding.\n\n4. **Should `complete-implementation`'s Pre-Phase-1 check also be fixed?** Fact-Check VERIFIED that line 318 mislabels \"artifact absent\" as \"no structured criteria,\" conflating two causes. Enforcement at plan-write time makes that conflation much less likely to occur, but does not make the logic correct \u2014 a TN task that exists but failed to register its artifact would still be misread. Whether that is in scope for #3277 or a separate follow-up is undecided. The item's Acceptance Criteria do not mention it.\n\n5. **How is the existing plan population assessed?** No enumeration of which of the 21 stored plans would newly fail validation has been performed. That count is the input to questions 1 and 2 and should be produced before the enforcement mode is chosen.\n\n---\n\n## Replacement Coverage Analysis\n\n**Not applicable.** This work does not replace or migrate a local module to an external tool, service, or library. It wires an existing in-repo validator (`sam_schema/core/dependencies.py::BookendValidator`) into existing in-repo write paths (`dh_core/operations.py`). Nothing is removed, no capability changes owner, and no external dependency is introduced or retired. This section is deliberately skipped rather than left blank.\n\n---\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Changing the plan file format or adding schema fields. `is-bookend` and `bookend-type` already exist and are documented in `docs/TASK_FILE_FORMAT.md` (lines 292-293, 388-389).\n- Duplicating validation into the three backends. The Impact Radius is explicit that the fix belongs above that layer.\n- Modifying `tests_sam/test_dependencies.py`. Acceptance Criteria #6 requires it to keep passing unmodified.\n- Changing `BookendValidator`'s rule set. Its logic is confirmed correct in isolation; the defect is that it never runs.\n\n---\n\n## Evidence Trail\n\nAll claims above are sourced from item #3277's groomed sections and from direct re-reads performed during this pass:\n\n- **Root-Cause Analysis** (item #3277) \u2014 the five-claim chain and the \"code/schema gap\" verdict.\n- **Fact-Check** (item #3277) \u2014 VERIFIED status for `Pb31107bf`'s 6 structured criteria, its `T1-T6`-only task list with all `bookend_type: null`, and `complete-implementation/SKILL.md:300-324`'s conflation of artifact-absent with no-criteria.\n- **Impact Radius** (item #3277) \u2014 the 16-system inventory, the single-source-of-truth constraint on where the fix belongs, and the generated-artifact warning.\n- **Issue Classification** (item #3277) \u2014 defect, with the Phase 4 Step 12 gate failure as scenario target.\n- **Design Intent Alignment** (item #3277) \u2014 MISSION_ALIGNED, citing README \u00a796 and commit `5b150f7ac`.\n- **Expected Behavior / Acceptance Criteria / Benefits / Dependencies** (item #3277).\n- **Direct re-read, this pass**: `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/dependencies.py:276-391` (validator present, five rules as described); `grep -rn \"BookendValidator\" --include=\"*.py\"` excluding tests returns only `sam_schema/__init__.py:30`, `sam_schema/__init__.py:71`, and `sam_schema/core/dependencies.py:276` \u2014 no caller; `sam_plan(action=\"list\")` returns 21 stored plans.\n\n**Note on RT-ICA**: the item's RT-ICA section entry is struck (superseded). Its two MISSING conditions \u2014 \"why did the planner skip generation\" and \"instruction-following vs. code/schema gap\" \u2014 were both subsequently answered by the Root-Cause Analysis, which is the current authority. Read the RT-ICA section as history, not as an open gap list.\n"}